00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.640
things like agility, the high
availability, the disaster

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:45.990
recovery--

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.810
critical, again, for
SAP environments.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.740
And then the capabilities of
a digital business platform,

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.980
a true digital platform around
analytics and machine learning

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.240
and artificial intelligence.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:02.430
And then lastly, Google Cloud's
commitment to your success--

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:08.000
this is not an easy
adventure, not an easy project

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.390
to move a mission-critical
environment like SAP

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.160
to the cloud.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.020
And we have a lot
of things in place

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:19.040
to help you mitigate risk
and help you to get there.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.250
So jumping into the
network and security,

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:27.320
I just want to start by
saying that, as a baseline,

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.290
Google Cloud is battle
tested from the get

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.320
go for performance, for
reliability, for security.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:37.880
It is already running
the same environment,

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.310
delivering applications at
planet-scale, applications

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:41.870
that you know, many
that you probably

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.480
use, like G Suite
and Maps and YouTube

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.880
and Search and
Android and others,

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.400
where the environment
is delivering

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:54.170
planet-scale, robust, secure
infrastructure already

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:57.380
for these billions of users
that are using these key Google

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.670
applications every day.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:06.020
So our security
schema or environment

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:09.990
really is around defense
at depth and at scale.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:15.050
And that means that every
element of the architecture

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:19.010
has security baked
in, essentially.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:22.880
And we build security
through progressive layers.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.690
Mostly, we build
everything ourselves.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.410
We buy very, very little
hardware in the end.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.047
Servers, for example, we're the
second or third largest server

00:03:30.047 --> 00:03:31.130
manufacturer in the world.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:35.000
And we're the only
customer of our servers.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.460
Our data centers, we build
them from the ground up.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:41.180
And boards, for example, they
have custom chips on board

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:43.850
that allow them to be
constantly monitoring, looking

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:49.400
for intrusions, looking for
possible changes or problem

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:50.120
areas.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.040
So every layer of
the environment

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.130
has security built in.

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:59.750
Another key component of
security is data encryption.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:03.860
And this is a unique Google
Cloud feature in the sense

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:08.180
that encryption is done by
default for data and packets

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:09.750
in transit and at rest.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.350
And there's nothing you need to
do to take advantage of that.

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:13.970
That's by default.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:18.709
So we don't see your data.

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:20.060
We don't reuse drives.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:21.470
If a customer were
to move on, we

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.450
would destroy a
physical hard drive.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:25.970
We would never reuse
a physical hard drive.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.887
When it comes to the keys
for your environment,

00:04:28.887 --> 00:04:30.470
you have complete
control of the keys.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.053
You keep your own
keys if you want to.

00:04:32.053 --> 00:04:37.400
We can hold your keys for you
if you want us to do that.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.550
So you are in control of
those critical security

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:44.330
elements of your environment.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:47.450
From a network
perspective, there's

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:49.040
lots of-- probably
lots of pictures

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:52.100
that look just like
this one, with the globe

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:54.650
and dots and lines and wires.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:56.890
But the difference with
Google Cloud's network

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:58.380
is a couple of points.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:59.720
First of all, it's ours.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:00.780
We built it.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:01.910
We own it.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:05.210
All the blue lines, those
undersea cables, they're ours.

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:05.840
We own them.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.660
We own the ships
and the submarines

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:11.420
that maintain and manage
those cables, that

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:14.790
have put those cables
under the ocean floor.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:17.872
So we have full control
over the network.

00:05:17.872 --> 00:05:19.580
And that's pretty
important, because when

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:22.610
we send your data from
one region to another,

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:25.340
we do not traverse
the public network.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.530
So this is both a security
enhancement for you

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:30.250
as well as a
performance enhancement.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:35.780
It gives you the lowest
latency possible for your SAP

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:36.980
applications.

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:38.470
And the network does matter.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:40.970
The network matters
tremendously for securities.

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:45.718
And the top here, there's
a typical schema going on,

00:05:45.718 --> 00:05:47.510
what we call the hot
potato syndrome, where

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:49.910
basically one of your
data packets gets

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:54.560
tossed around, essentially,
from one network to another

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.470
on its way to its
final destination.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.118
In Google Cloud,
that never happens.

00:05:58.118 --> 00:05:59.660
Essentially, again,
we send your data

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:03.440
from one point to another,
one region to another

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:07.710
without traversing
the public networks.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:10.060
So big advantages
for SAP customers.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:13.100
Take, again, HADR,
for example, where

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:16.460
you can host your
high-availability systems

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:19.340
in different
locations in a region

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:22.700
and ensure synchronous
updates that

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:26.690
are sub-millisecond, extremely
fast, low-latency updates.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.610
For DR, for disaster
recovery, we

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:32.600
can provision DR sites
that are milliseconds away.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.650
So we'll come back to
HA and DR and talk more

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:40.610
about that, as critical as
it is for SAP customers.

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:42.500
So moving on now to
smart infrastructure--

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:44.780
so augmenting the
baseline of security,

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:48.710
the baseline of the
network with capabilities

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:54.450
like agility and more
HA and DR capabilities.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:07:00.190
So the base here for our
Google Cloud SAP infrastructure

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:02.490
is a virtualized environment.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:04.560
We made the decision three
years back or so when

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.470
we decided to host SAP
environments that we would

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.630
be making an investment
in virtual machines,

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:13.020
and very large virtual
machines for SAP systems.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:16.170
And that's two advantages
that it offers customers.

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:18.810
One is that you don't have
to get it right in sizing.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:22.140
Even though you do, you run
the tools and the scenarios

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:24.747
and you choose the size of
your initial environment,

00:07:24.747 --> 00:07:25.830
it's hard to get it right.

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.413
But you don't have
to on Google Cloud,

00:07:27.413 --> 00:07:30.810
because once you get here, you
can quickly adjust and adjust

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:32.010
without penalty.

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.600
And then go forward, you
can resize without penalty,

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:37.560
whether changing
times, changing things

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.840
going on in the market
and the environment.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:45.630
You can adjust and
resize your environment

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.038
with what the business demands.

00:07:47.038 --> 00:07:48.580
Or maybe on the
weekends or at night,

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:50.670
you want to turn stuff
off, like your dev or test

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.920
or non-production environments.

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:57.330
That's available to you, too,
to save money, save money

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:58.680
in compute instances.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:03.090
So global provisioning in
minutes, large VMs that

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:05.610
are in production in
customer environments,

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:10.380
and more-- and more investment
coming in this area in future.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:13.650
So a key capability that
we are able to offer

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:19.620
in the high-availability
space around planned downtime

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:20.550
is live migration.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:22.050
And this is another
thing that we're

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.760
able to offer because of
our VM-based environment.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:28.290
And essentially, what
this is is it's--

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:30.510
live migration keeps
virtual machine instances

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:33.039
up and running through
planned host system events,

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:35.640
like a hypervisor
update, like a like

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:39.669
a patch, a basic hardware
or hardware patch.

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:41.220
And this is big
for SAP customers,

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:45.090
when some can't even afford to
take a system down for an hour,

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:48.060
a month, because that lost
downtime is something that they

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:50.543
simply cannot afford.

00:08:50.543 --> 00:08:51.960
But the way it
works, essentially,

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:53.970
is you take your
productive live--

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:58.830
your productive environment
and move it to another VM,

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:02.010
keep running, certainly
no big reboots required.

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:04.830
And update the
original environment.

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:07.140
Patch and update as needed.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:10.390
And then you roll back when
the update is completed.

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:14.520
So live migrating, live
instances without reboots,

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.940
performing infrastructure
updates without disruption--

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:20.190
and then we, too,
Google Cloud, take

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:21.900
advantage of this
capability to make sure

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:24.090
that your environment
is always up to date.

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:27.360
We're not trading off
downtime and updates.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:29.370
We're keeping it
updated all the time.

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:30.750
And we'll never
send you an email

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:33.333
saying the environment's going
to go down on Sunday afternoon.

00:09:33.333 --> 00:09:36.150
You need to be prepared,
because we don't take down

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:36.910
our environment.

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:41.830
And you don't need to,
either, for doing these types

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:45.280
of maintenance updates.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:48.420
All right, so moving
on more around

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.450
availability, HA and DR--

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:54.300
so this is so important for
SAP customers to get right.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.670
Again, you architect it rarely.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:00.240
But when you have to use it,
it really needs to be right.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.630
Most of the environments look
like on the left of this page,

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:07.740
where your HA environment is
spread over multiple VPCs,

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.610
virtual private clouds.

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:12.570
And that's not great,
from even in good times.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.970
It introduces additional
points of entry

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:16.680
from a security risk standpoint.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:20.340
But then even concerning
more is if you have a failure

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:23.378
and you have to actually
failover to a secondary system,

00:10:23.378 --> 00:10:25.170
it could take a lot of
time, because you've

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:28.200
got to inform the downstream
systems that IP addresses have

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:29.040
changed, et cetera.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:33.400
So all your upstream-downstream
systems have to be made aware.

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:35.820
And this could take
hours in some cases.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:38.830
In the Google Cloud
setup on the right,

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:41.000
it's completely different.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:47.880
Your HA can be configured
within the same VPC.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:51.800
So lets say, in the
event of a failed system,

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:53.907
you failover to a secondary,
which was already up

00:10:53.907 --> 00:10:54.990
and running to begin with.

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:57.180
You failover to your secondary.

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:59.638
And basically, it's
synchronously updated.

00:10:59.638 --> 00:11:00.930
And you're back up and running.

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:03.840
You're failed over
within seconds,

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:05.730
without impacting
upstream-downstream

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:06.480
applications.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.342
There's none of
that IP remapping

00:11:08.342 --> 00:11:09.300
that has to take place.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.660
You don't have to send resets.

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:14.010
There's no DNS table updates.

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:15.180
It's business continuity.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.250
It's business
continuity maintained

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:22.500
for a mission-critical
environment.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:25.350
So I want to share with you
all here not just me talking,

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.870
but from one of our customers,
Cardinal Health, one

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:31.090
of the largest pharmaceutical
distributors in the United

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:32.370
States.

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:35.310
And they've been running
live SAP HANA on Google Cloud

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:36.990
for about nine months or so.

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.870
And I'd like you to take a
listen to their experiences

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:42.463
running SAP on Google Cloud.

00:11:42.463 --> 00:11:47.560
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:13:37.201 --> 00:13:38.140
[END PLAYBACK]

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:42.130
All right, so moving on to
the digital business platform,

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:44.140
analytics capabilities,
machine learning--

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:48.070
some of these real game-changing
type of technologies

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:49.410
for your SAP environment.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:53.170
They're easier than you think,
actually, to get on board with

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:54.920
and deploy.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.820
So how does Google Cloud really
innovate for SAP customers?

00:13:57.820 --> 00:13:59.840
A number of areas that
I want to cover for you.

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:02.720
First, on the left
here is actually

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.280
containerization
with Kubernetes,

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:08.620
something so important for
customers, for SAP customers,

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:11.840
where, like it or not, for
the foreseeable future,

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:13.870
this is multi-cloud
and hybrid cloud.

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:17.590
And even if you're able to
move your SAP, your primary SAP

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:19.930
environment to the
cloud, you still

00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:22.300
have to deal with
upstream-downstream

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:23.590
applications.

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:25.330
Some will be still
on-prem for a while.

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:26.200
Some will be hybrid.

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:27.920
Some will be in other clouds.

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:30.640
So how do you take all
of these applications

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:33.970
and elegantly containerize
them and move ahead

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:35.850
without fear of
the hybrid nature

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:38.660
or the multi-cloud nature
of the world we're in?

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:42.010
And the answer for
that is Kubernetes

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:44.110
for containerization,
and then Google Cloud

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:49.780
Anthos to help you to build,
deploy, and manage applications

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:53.570
in this multi-cloud,
hybrid cloud world.

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:55.780
There's also Apigee, which is--

00:14:55.780 --> 00:15:01.030
really, for SAP customers, takes
care of a really key concern

00:15:01.030 --> 00:15:03.940
in the area of innovation, where
so many customers are concerned

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:05.920
about integration testing.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:08.080
This is like--
becomes the worry when

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:11.100
they want to explore
new areas of innovation.

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:13.225
Then when they go to test
and do integration tests,

00:15:13.225 --> 00:15:15.267
they're worried they're
going to break something.

00:15:15.267 --> 00:15:17.470
They're going to break
a downstream or upstream

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:18.470
